    "math_academy_id": "q-75314",
    "knowledge_point_id": "3f41b2bd-0c1d-5267-b91d-7aba35ea7efd",
    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function From Its Graph",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14096835/assets/a55c30803fa9e8351613c39c20133f8fc31045ff46fb4e11b63eeca10f9234c2.png)\n\nConsider the graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
    "worked_solution": "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0]$  and the line segment for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4+1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
